% ============================================================================
% radius-definition — what "radius" measures for a circle vs. a sphere, per
% the SAME already-cited MathWorld sentence (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOMETRY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `circle-parts.adj` (`circle_part(part,
% description)`, keyed by PART of a circle: radius, diameter, circumference,
% chord). This table recalls a DIFFERENT axis the SAME already-cited
% MathWorld "Radius" page states in ONE sentence: what "radius" measures for
% each of TWO shapes — not just the circle `circle-parts.adj` already tables,
% but the sphere the same sentence also names. `circle-parts.adj`'s own
% `source` field already quotes the full sentence — "The distance from the
% center of a circle to its perimeter, OR FROM THE CENTER OF A SPHERE TO ITS
% SURFACE" — but that table's `part`-keyed schema has no row for a shape
% other than circle, so the sphere half never became a row. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? radius_definition(sphere, $D)      % center_to_surface
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table keyed by
% SHAPE rather than by circle-part): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `radius_definition(shape, description)` carrying the citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% defining phrase WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on any solid this source's own sentence does not name (a cube, a
% cone, a cylinder — a radius is not even the standard measure for those
% shapes, and this sentence never claims otherwise).
%
% The two shapes and the defining phrase the SAME sentence states for each,
% as a little truth table:
%
%     shape    what "radius" measures (MathWorld)          token
%     ------   ------------------------------------------  -------------------
%     circle   "from the center of a circle to its         center_to_perimeter
%              perimeter"
%     sphere   "from the center of a sphere to its         center_to_surface
%              surface"
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the shape it
% defines:
%
%     ? radius_definition($Shape, center_to_perimeter)   % circle
%
% Only these two shapes appear in the cited sentence; a solid this sentence
% does not name — cube, cone, cylinder — has no row here, so a recall on it
% ABSTAINS rather than inventing a definition. That honest-abstention
% property is what makes the table safe to reason from.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME sentence already quoted
% inside `circle-parts.adj`'s own provenance block (its `source` field, in
% fact — not even the header), from the SAME MathWorld "Radius" page that
% table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a different schema (shape →
% description, not part → description) decoding the SECOND clause of a
% sentence that table's first row already used the FIRST clause of. An ADJ
% `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so `source`/`locator`/`trust`
% below hold the full two-clause sentence — the single span that fixes both
% rows at once. MathWorld (mathworld.wolfram.com) is a primary mathematics
% reference, the same tier `circle-parts.adj` already uses, so `trust
% authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table radius_definition {
    columns shape, description

    row (circle, center_to_perimeter)
    row (sphere, center_to_surface)

    source "The distance from the center of a circle to its perimeter, or from the center of a sphere to its surface."
    locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Radius.html"
    trust authoritative
}
